                  <provenance type="#c"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_4">Canterbury, Kent.
                        Benedictine cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ
                        Church</orgName><p>Before All Souls (see below), the manuscript had
                        apparently been at Christ Church Canterbury and is in a Canterbury binding
                        of c. 1450–1500. William Chart O.S.B., monk of Christ Church, is a
                        correspondent in the second section. For the whole contents see a short note
                        by E. L[obel], 'A fifteenth century MS. in private hands', Bodl Quarterly
                        Record 5, pp. 134–59; and R. Weiss, 'Some unpublished correspondence of
                        Guarino da Verona', Italian Studies (1939), pp. 110–113.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_425">Oxford, Oxfordshire. All
                        Souls' College</orgName><p><q>Liber Collegii animarum omnium fidelium
                           defunctorum Ex nota Reverendissimi patris <persName role="dnr">Iacobi
                              Goldwell</persName> Norwicensis episcopi et legum doctoris ac quondam
                           huius collegii] socii</q> (erased but legible under u. v.
                     light).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Canon B. H. Streeter, Provost of Queen's
                     College and New Testament scholar (d. 1937), who presented the book to the
                     Imperial University, Tokyo. The index card originally recorded a date of
                     'c.1923' for the donation. However, this was later crossed out and another hand
                     added the following clarification: 'BQR article published second quarter of
                     1927 says manuscript in Streeter's hands, and Streeter notes BQR ref on a
                     flyleaf.'</provenance>
               </history>
